db-ai — SQLite databases as a virtual filesystem

Usage:
  db-ai <command> [flags] [arguments]
  db-ai --help

Commands:
  version
      print the functional version and build metadata
  mount FILE /ALIAS [--mode ro|rw]
      register a SQLite file under an alias
  umount /ALIAS
      remove a mount; never deletes the file
  mounts
      list registered mounts
  ls [PATH] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
      list children of a path
  tree [PATH] [--depth N] [--limit N]
      list descendants up to a depth
  stat PATH
      show what a path is
  describe PATH
      describe a table, its columns and capabilities
  cat PATH [--fields COL,...]
      read a row or a field as JSON
  cd PATH
      change the session working directory
  pwd
      print the session working directory
  find PATH [--where EXPR]... [--order COL[:asc|desc],...] [--fields COL,...] [--count] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
      filter rows with --where expressions
  grep PATH TEXT [--count] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
      search a literal substring in text columns
  touch PATH --data JSON|-
      insert a row, returning its key
  write ROW_OR_FIELD --data JSON|- --if-match ETAG
      update a field or a row with an ETag precondition
  upsert TABLE --data JSON|- [--if-match ETAG] [--dry-run]
      create or update one row by its primary key, reporting which branch ran
  cp ROW DEST_ROW --if-match ETAG
      copy a row within its table
  mv ROW DEST_ROW --if-match ETAG --apply
      rename a simple primary key
  rm ROW --if-match ETAG --apply
      delete one row
  batch --data JSON|-
      apply several mutations in one transaction
  export PATH [--where EXPR]... [--format json|jsonl] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
      export rows to stdout as JSON or JSONL
  serve [--port N] [--open]
      serve the read-only local explorer over HTTP

Global flags:
  --json           accepted for compatibility; JSON is the only output format
  --session ID     session ID that resolves relative paths
  --timeout N      operation timeout in seconds, 1-60 (default 5)
  --actor NAME     informational actor label
  --request-id ID  client request ID; generated when absent
  --help           show help (-h also works)

Output:
  Exactly one JSON object per invocation on stdout:
    success: {"version":1,"ok":true,"data":...,"meta":{"truncated":false,"request_id":"..."}}
    failure: {"version":1,"ok":false,"error":{"code":"E_...","message":"..."}}
  Diagnostics go to stderr.

Exit codes:
  0    OK
  2    E_USAGE
  3    E_NOT_FOUND or E_STALE_SESSION
  4    E_DENIED
  5    E_CONFLICT
  6    E_CONSTRAINT
  7    E_BUSY or E_TIMEOUT
  8    E_UNSUPPORTED or E_LIMIT
  9    E_INTERNAL
  10   E_COMMIT_UNKNOWN

Examples:
  db-ai mount ./tasks.db /work
  db-ai ls /work
  db-ai cat /work/tasks/TK-001
  db-ai find /work/tasks --where 'status=pending' --limit 20
